 * @section xpost_intro Introduction
 *
 * This is xpost-BWIPP, hereafter "xpost": a PostScript Level-3
 * interpreter based on xpost by M Joshua Ryan (luser droog). The
 * original design has been extensively reworked and extended to serve
 * the needs of Barcode Writer in Pure PostScript.
 *
 * The PostScript Language Reference Manual and the Technical Notes that
 * follow it are the sole design authority. Other interpreters serve
 * only as black-box oracles. Where behaviour departs from the
 * specification, COMPLIANCE records it.
 *
 * xpost is built as a shared library (libxpost) with a command-line
 * interpreter and an embedding example on top of it. README.md at the
 * tree root describes what it implements, its output devices, and how
 * it is tested. doc/COMPAT records where each user-visible name comes
 * from.
 *
 * Development has largely been vibe coded against specifications
 * derived from the reference. At this point it is highly experimental
 * and should not be used on untrusted inputs.
 *
 * @section xpost_compilation Compilation and installation
 *
 * The primary build is meson:
 *
 * @verbatim
meson setup builddir
meson compile -C builddir
meson install -C builddir
   @endverbatim
 *
 * The tree also carries an autotools build (./autogen.sh, ./configure,
 * make, make install), which builds the same programs. README.md
 * documents both, and @ref xpost_design describes how the interpreter
 * works, from the arena up.
 *
 * This reference is generated by doxygen from the library and program
 * sources; the make target is @c doc under autotools, and the @c doc
 * custom target under meson.
 *
 * @section xpost_thanks Where this came from
 *
 * The development of xpost benefited greatly from the support and
 * advice of fellow programmers on usenet in comp.lang.c,
 * comp.lang.postscript and comp.unix.programmer, and from questions
 * and answers on Stack Overflow. The contributors are named in AUTHORS,
 * and individual files bear the copyright of their authors.
